{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Persistently disable IPv4 ICMP redirect and source-route acceptance on node1.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A root-owned sysctl configuration disables both settings for all, default, and wildcard interface scopes. Every current IPv4 scope was zero immediately after application and remained zero after a verified reboot, directly demonstrating persistence.","evidence_ids":["cmd-00f064a0f14143eb","cmd-5e1081faf4704175"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Retain the installed operating-system release and kernel and use a compatible implementation.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Before and after execution, node1 remained on Red Hat Enterprise Linux 10.0 with kernel 6.12.0-55.9.1.el10_0.x86_64. The implementation used the platform-native sysctl mechanism and did not install or replace software.","evidence_ids":["cmd-87427b4160d44e93","cmd-5e1081faf4704175","global:before_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform"]}],"confidence":1,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified. Creating the sysctl file (cmd-495e6af4bbab4589) and restarting systemd-sysctl (cmd-96a7204cc2e24f8e) directly established the required hardening; scheduling the reboot (cmd-410de97c7dff456c) fulfilled the mandatory restart protocol and validated persistence. The remaining commands were observations or expected connection failures during reboot. Afterward there were no failed services, package damage, new exposure, or unexplained residue; socket and systemd-private-directory differences were normal reboot effects.","evidence_ids":["cmd-495e6af4bbab4589","cmd-96a7204cc2e24f8e","cmd-410de97c7dff456c","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. The hardening was directly verified before and after reboot, while the OS release and kernel remained unchanged. The provider-wide restart protocol is complete: healthy pre-restart state and boot ID, restart acknowledgement, a different post-restart boot ID, and recovered system, networking, persistent configuration, and connectivity were captured. Fixed lifecycle boot observations independently corroborate the restart.","limitations":[]}
